WEBVTT

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:08.140
The natural world is full of extraordinary animals with amazing life histories.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.580
Yet certain stories are more intriguing than most.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:22.020
The mysteries of a butterfly's life cycle, or the strange biology of the emperor penguin.

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:28.830
Some of these creatures were surrounded by myths and misunderstandings for a very long time.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:33.260
and some have only recently revealed their secrets.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.880
These are the animals that stand out from the crowd,

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.690
the curiosities I find most fascinating of all.

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:54.930
Spiders spin intricate webs using their own silk,

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:59.050
and birds weave nests from strips of leaves.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:04.550
In this program, I investigate the skill of these spinners and weavers

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:10.050
and the way they use such materials to produce such truly complex structures.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:24.740
Birds build a variety of nests, each with a design that is characteristic of their species.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.520
The simplest nests are just sticks wedged into position.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.540
But some are more complicated.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:38.140
The long-tailed tit builds a delicate nest from plant material and spider silk.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.640
And weaver birds do, literally, weave with leaves.

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:50.110
But are such skills learned or instinctive?

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:59.960
In 1905, Eugène Marais, a South African writer and scientist, was intrigued by the...

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.360
complexity of weaver bird nests. He wanted to understand more about their nest building

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:11.240
skills and performed a rigorous but simple experiment to see if they learnt how to make

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:17.730
nests or if they built them using what he called cultural instinct. He took eggs from

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:25.400
a pair of wild weaver birds and put them into a canary's nest to hatch. Then he encouraged

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:29.960
the next three generations of weaver birds to breed but gave them

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.120
no nest material and hatched their eggs, once again, under canaries.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:39.760
When nesting time came for the fourth generation of weaver birds, he gave them natural nest

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:46.040
materials and, without hesitation, they vigorously set about constructing perfect wild nests.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:55.790
So nest building is largely under genetic control, but it is influenced by experience

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:56.950
and the environment.

00:02:58.060 --> 00:02:59.960
nests of the same kind of weaver

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.740
bird are not always exactly the same and the birds of necessity must have some flexibility in how they

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:14.740
build. Nests that hang are particularly difficult to make as the birds have to work against gravity

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:21.110
with no support from below. Weaver birds solve part of this problem with a skill none others

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:29.250
have. They're the only birds that can tie knots. These knots vary and are worked on until the weaver

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:29.970
Succeeds in

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:38.260
attaching several strands of grass to a suitable branch or stem. These first

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:42.610
fastenings are crucial as the whole of the completed nest will hang from them.

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:52.340
Once the birds have secured the foundation they can start to weave.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:03:59.920
Weaving is just one

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.640
way of binding leaves together.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:04.540
There are others.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:10.760
These are tailor-bird nests.

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:17.640
They consist of folded leaves stuffed with soft material and stitched together using spider silk.

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:21.380
The tailor bird pierces the leaves with its sharp beak

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:24.480
and then binds them together by pulling silk through the holes.

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:29.670
The complete operation involves a number of different skills.

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:33.850
Making the holes is like riveting.

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:39.780
Two leaves are placed together and then pierced to create matching holes above and below.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:45.540
Then the edges are sewn up.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:50.440
The upper surface of the leaf is kept to the outside.

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:53.390
to help the nest look unobtrusive.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.600
The result is a secure pocket.

00:04:58.890 --> 00:04:59.850
which is then stuffed.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:00.960
with a soft lining.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:12.560
The materials the birds choose to sew up their nests can vary.

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:16.940
At the turn of the century, there was a report in The Common Birds of Bombay

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:22.220
of weaver birds watching carpet makers and tailors as they worked on verandas.

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:29.510
When the coast was clear, the birds flew down and stole tiny pieces of thread with which to sew up their nests.

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:38.300
The birds search with a clear idea of what would be suitable nest material, many use sticks

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:39.240
and twigs.

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:49.640
They will, however, occasionally use other material that does the same job, and their

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:51.500
choices are sometimes surprising.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:59.980
This nest was found in an aircraft hangar in the 1950s, and it's made in

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:01.840
entirely of twisted wire.

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:06.520
When it was discovered, it contained two blood bird eggs.

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:09.260
It's an unusual nest for a blackbird.

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:13.060
But similar nests have been found belonging to crows and pigeons.

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:20.140
Feverbirds work with natural material.

00:06:20.630 --> 00:06:24.890
And like the tailor bird, they have to solve the problem of joining leaves together.

00:06:26.230 --> 00:06:29.170
After making a knot to secure the basic framework,

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:29.980
They be.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:30.720
Again, they're weaving.

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:35.090
They construct the main egg chamber...

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:40.700
and then add a small entrance around the first securely knotted ring of leaves.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:47.680
The male, as he works, is under intense scrutiny.

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:51.580
Females are looking for mates.

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:55.080
and males that build firm, well-positioned nests...

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:56.570
are favoured as fathers.

00:06:58.440 --> 00:06:59.940
When he finishes...

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.800
Mail advertises his handiwork by fluttering.

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:07.000
but he may be forced to build several nests.

00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:09.550
before a female finally chooses him

00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:10.590
as a partner.

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:16.090
Weaverbird's nests are very conspicuous.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:21.180
Other birds, however, go to some trouble to conceal them.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:28.020
We may not have tailor birds or weaver birds in Britain.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:29.940
But we do have loans.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:30.820
tail tits.

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:35.440
delicate little birds that make intricate and finely constructed nests.

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:42.930
With tiny, repetitive movements, they use loops of spider silk

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:46.490
to felt together a mixture of wool and moss.

00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:57.960
both male and female.

00:07:58.250 --> 00:07:59.570
Work on the construction.

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:08.200
As the nest takes shape, they decorate the outside with several thousand tiny flakes of lichen.

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:27.120
The nest is then lined with hundreds of feathers and provides a delicate but strong structure to house the growing chicks.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:35.610
And it's a nest that's particularly hard to find because of its covering of lichen.

00:08:39.790 --> 00:08:44.480
For years it was believed that this acted as a sort of camouflage to help hide the nest.

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:51.120
But the recent discovery of long-tailed tit nests covered with small flakes of paper and polystyrene

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:55.030
have helped explain more clearly the reason for this decoration.

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:59.480
Rather than helping to blend the nest with its background,

00:08:59.580 --> 00:08:59.940
these...

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.020
small flakes reflect light from it, making it almost invisible.

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:08.730
And it seems paper and polystyrene do the job just as well as lichen.

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:16.740
The largest and perhaps the most long-lasting nest of all

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:19.240
is made by the social weaver bird.

00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:25.540
They live in the dry areas of southern Africa

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:29.970
and work together to build what looks like a great haystack up in...

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:39.030
tree. New nest chambers are continually added. As many as a hundred pairs of birds may live

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:52.420
together under the one roof, as you might say. Chambers provide shade during the day and keep

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:53.760
out the chill at night.

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:09.580
And the whole construction is so robust that it may provide mass housing for generation after generation of birds.

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:27.360
Recently, the biggest nest ever recorded was discovered attached to telegraph poles in the Kalahari Desert.

00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:29.710
It's more than seven meters across.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:37.200
three meters high. So weaver birds make their nests in many different ways and it

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:41.760
was once thought that they worked entirely by instinct but this is not so.

00:10:43.150 --> 00:10:48.170
They are amongst the most expert nest builders in the animal kingdom and this

00:10:48.170 --> 00:10:52.530
array of nests shows the complex and elaborate designs that they can

00:10:52.530 --> 00:10:57.300
produce. Recent studies suggest that weaver birds may be using mental skills

00:10:57.300 --> 00:10:59.980
that are not dissimilar to those required to make

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:09.010
simple tools for weaver birds a well-built nest is a ticket to successful breeding you would

00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:17.420
imagine that such complexity could be produced using just a foot and a beak in part two we

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:23.300
see how spiders use silk a substance that they don't find in their surroundings but make themselves

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:29.960
Infobirds are skilled builders who create one

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:32.960
wonderfully elaborate nests from the simplest material.

00:11:35.550 --> 00:11:38.800
Spiders, on the other hand, construct their intricate webs

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:43.460
from a material they produce from their own bodies, silk.

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:55.320
Spider silk is unique. It's very thin, very strong,

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:57.960
and has many exciting potential uses.

00:11:59.130 --> 00:11:59.970
Spiders spin it with...

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.220
these but scientists have been trying to copy it for many years. To do that we

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:09.580
need to understand two of the spider's secrets. The exact structure and nature of

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:16.840
their silk and the way they transform it from a fluid into a thread. Spider silk

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:22.680
is a truly remarkable material. It can withstand impact and it can be strong

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:28.860
stretchy and sticky all at the same time. Spiders produce it from special

00:12:28.860 --> 00:12:29.740
glands inside.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:35.840
bodies and extrude it from tiny nipples called spinnerets at the back end of their abdomens.

00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:42.120
And what is more, they can produce up to seven different kinds, each with its own purpose.

00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:47.800
For centuries, it was the only silk known to man.

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:53.610
The ancient Greeks used cobwebs to stop bleeding, and Australian Aborigines used it

00:12:53.610 --> 00:12:54.870
to catch small fish.

00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:59.750
Then, in the Far East, a different and mysterious

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.960
new kind of silk started to appear and in much larger quantities. According to

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:11.930
Chinese legend, the first person to weave silk into a fabric was the Empress Lai

00:13:11.930 --> 00:13:18.060
Zhu back in the 27th century BC. She was having tea in her garden under a

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:24.480
mulberry tree when a cocoon fell from the branch above and dropped into her cup

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:29.970
and started to unravel. Whether that's true or not, the Empress Lai Zhu is now

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:36.060
honored as the goddess of silk and silk moth farming dates back to the beginning of Chinese

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:43.320
civilization the silk was traded right across the Near East and into the Roman Empire the Chinese

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:51.180
traders were sworn to secrecy about how this marvelous material was made but in the year 532

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:58.510
the Roman Emperor Justinian managed to find out that it came not as some suspected from a spider's

00:13:58.510 --> 00:13:59.910
web but from the

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:08.860
cocoon of a moth. Silk moth caterpillars produce large quantities of silk and they make it in a

00:14:08.860 --> 00:14:14.580
very different way to spiders. The caterpillars feed voraciously on mulberry leaves and then

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:20.330
when they're full grown and ready to transform into a moth they spin silk and cocoons in which

00:14:20.330 --> 00:14:27.170
they will pupate. Unlike spiders which have specialized spinning organs, silk moth caterpillars

00:14:27.170 --> 00:14:29.390
produce silk from their salivary gland.

00:14:31.850 --> 00:14:37.930
Each cocoon is made from a single unbroken filament that can be over 500 meters long.

00:14:38.940 --> 00:14:42.380
This silk is plentiful and easy to spin commercially.

00:14:43.180 --> 00:14:45.740
But it isn't as tough as spider silk.

00:14:50.020 --> 00:14:53.580
And spider silk also has more exciting potential uses.

00:14:57.800 --> 00:14:59.960
An orb-web like this is constructed...

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:08.050
over a Y-shaped scaffold of silk threads which are extremely strong. Unlike silk worms, the female

00:15:08.050 --> 00:15:14.590
spiders which spin the webs are very territorial and aggressive, so farming and collecting spider

00:15:14.590 --> 00:15:24.600
silk is very difficult, but it has been done. In 1762, a Spanish missionary called Tamaya

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:29.310
made a machine that held a single spider from which he pulled a silken thread.

00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:36.730
In London, Daniel Roelt, a factory worker, attached spiders to a small steam machine

00:15:36.730 --> 00:15:40.590
and succeeded in reeling out 18 meters of silk a minute.

00:15:42.020 --> 00:15:46.340
That led to machines which were able to milk several spiders at a time.

00:15:50.500 --> 00:15:56.360
Experiments then stopped until 2004, when two textile artists in Madagascar

00:15:56.420 --> 00:15:59.940
built a machine based on these early designs with which...

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.200
they made something very special indeed.

00:16:04.680 --> 00:16:08.540
The golden colour of this stunningly beautiful spider silk shawl

00:16:08.540 --> 00:16:10.180
is completely natural.

00:16:11.080 --> 00:16:16.640
The silk from which it was made was produced by 1,063,000 spiders,

00:16:16.850 --> 00:16:19.640
like this one, over four years.

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:24.170
Local people collected 3,000 spiders a day,

00:16:24.410 --> 00:16:29.290
and trained handlers extracted silk from groups of 24 at a time.

00:16:30.090 --> 00:16:33.670
After being milked, the spiders were released back into the wild.

00:16:34.730 --> 00:16:37.950
The individual silk strands were then twisted into a thread,

00:16:38.190 --> 00:16:42.560
which was woven into this intricately patterned fabric on looms.

00:16:46.710 --> 00:16:50.090
Now, this kind of silk fabric production couldn't work commercially.

00:16:50.650 --> 00:16:53.110
Apart from being hard work to make in quantity,

00:16:53.530 --> 00:16:57.310
spider silk isn't really a very suitable thread for fabric.

00:16:58.400 --> 00:16:59.980
As a cloth, it reacts better.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:05.700
badly to moisture and heat, but in its natural state as a single thread, it has physical

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:13.790
qualities that could be exploited medically. These special characteristics are a consequence

00:17:13.790 --> 00:17:20.660
of the molecular structure of spider silk. It consists of two large protein molecules.

00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:25.970
One is stretchy and spaghetti-like, and the other has a harder crystalline structure.

00:17:26.410 --> 00:17:29.750
Combined, these two proteins give silk unique...

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:36.580
qualities of strength and flexibility spiders store these proteins as a gel

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:40.880
like liquid in their bodies and when they need to make silk they extrude it

00:17:40.880 --> 00:17:47.140
through the spinnerets combining the molecules in a special way if we hold

00:17:47.140 --> 00:17:52.780
down a spider without harming it we can see this process in more detail normally

00:17:52.780 --> 00:17:57.700
the spider would attach the end of the silk filament to an object and then move

00:17:57.700 --> 00:17:59.900
away so that the filament is pulled from

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:05.460
spinnerets we can produce the same reaction by gently pulling the end of

00:18:05.460 --> 00:18:10.780
the filament itself internally the silk liquid is passing down a long duct in

00:18:10.780 --> 00:18:15.420
which stretchy elements within the protein molecules are lined with harder

00:18:15.420 --> 00:18:24.650
crystalline ones to create an extremely strong and tough thread scanning

00:18:24.650 --> 00:18:29.210
electron microscopes reveal how the liquid emerges from the spinnerets

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:37.540
Credibly, spiders can convert liquid proteins into a hardened thread at room temperature with very little energy.

00:18:38.230 --> 00:18:43.590
If we could understand and copy this process, it would be a major scientific breakthrough.

00:18:46.270 --> 00:18:51.610
Scientists have, in fact, spent many years trying to replicate the spider's liquid silk and the way it spun.

00:18:52.810 --> 00:18:59.800
Recently, the genes for spider silk proteins were cloned and put into goats to try and produce silk.

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:07.650
in their milk it worked and when the goats had kids silk proteins were extracted from the mother's

00:19:07.650 --> 00:19:17.000
milk but none of these processes have yet produced silk that is as tough as natural spider silk this

00:19:18.360 --> 00:19:24.300
machine is called a tensile tester and it shows how strong and stretchy spider silk can be

00:19:25.030 --> 00:19:28.960
This drag-lion silk is being pulled apart.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:35.700
shows the force the fiber is taking and at what point it breaks a steel thread of

00:19:35.700 --> 00:19:48.450
similar diameter would have broken by now there it's broken spider silk is the

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:57.340
toughest natural material known to man a single thread of web silk less than a

00:19:57.340 --> 00:19:59.980
millimeter thick can absorb the impact

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.900
of fast-moving prey and bring it to a halt without breaking.

00:20:05.080 --> 00:20:07.500
Complete webs can stretch enormously.

00:20:08.090 --> 00:20:10.230
and then return to their original shape.

00:20:10.520 --> 00:20:11.840
with a minimum of damage.

00:20:15.220 --> 00:20:20.040
Incredibly, spiders can make this complex material from just fresh air,

00:20:20.410 --> 00:20:21.670
Flies and water.

00:20:23.510 --> 00:20:26.130
The best we can do in making a material like it...

00:20:26.240 --> 00:20:27.260
requires oil

00:20:27.480 --> 00:20:29.640
chemicals and a great deal of energy.

00:20:32.810 --> 00:20:38.170
Although we now better understand the structure of spider silk and the natural spinning process...

00:20:38.340 --> 00:20:40.680
we still can't perform the spider's magic.

00:20:41.030 --> 00:20:43.130
and copy this extraordinary substance.

00:20:44.710 --> 00:20:48.630
But using small amounts of natural spider silk in clever ways...

00:20:48.740 --> 00:20:51.380
has nonetheless a very exciting future.

00:20:53.510 --> 00:20:55.390
A sumptuous golden cloth.

00:20:55.750 --> 00:20:57.610
It's just one possible product.

00:20:59.690 --> 00:20:59.950
This

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:06.130
This is a dream that has become a reality and shows just how lovely spider silk can be.

00:21:06.380 --> 00:21:11.640
But it also has the potential to make other dreams come true.

00:21:12.490 --> 00:21:14.610
It's a biodegradable material.

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:17.700
that we are now using to make artificial joints,

00:21:17.920 --> 00:21:21.760
and it may even help repair damaged spinal tissue.

00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:27.880
This curiosity of nature could eventually save lives.

00:21:35.300 --> 00:21:36.280
Thank you.
